280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Proxy wars are conflicts where major powers pursue strategic objectives indirectly by supporting other nations or non-state groups instead of engaging in direct military confrontation. This support could be through funding, weapons, training, intelligence, or logistical assistance. Proxy wars have become a central tool of geopolitical competition; by relying on proxies, states are able to secure access to strategic resources and expand regional influence while reducing costs and risks associated with direct conflict.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Competition over valuable resources such as water, gas, oil and rare earth minerals has increased and intensified existing conflicts, with major powers backing opposing sides to protect their own economic and geopolitical interests. Because of this, many proxy conflicts have been prolonged.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Proxy wars are known to destabilise entire regions, displace millions of people, destroy critical infrastructure and contribute to humanitarian crises, yet they continue to happen because international systems fail to hold these major powers and sponsors accountable for the destruction they finance.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (The United Nations has repeatedly addressed issues related to proxy warfare; however, no comprehensive international framework specifically regulates proxy warfare.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (One of the most notable proxy wars in history was the Cold War. Instead of engaging in direct military conflict, the Soviet Union and the United States supported opposing groups, governments, and liberation movements in conflicts such as the Vietnam War and the Korean War.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (These conflicts enabled both superpowers to expand their influence while avoiding the risk of conflict escalating into full-blown nuclear war. After the end of the Cold War, proxy wars did not disappear. Instead, proxy wars evolved as more and more regional and global powers began to rely on proxy forces to advance their political, economic, and military interests.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (In recent years, conflicts in countries such as Syria, Libya, and Yemen have demonstrated how proxy wars prolong violence and worsen humanitarian crises. Many foreign states have supported rival factions, escalating domestic conflicts into complex regional struggles. One example is the conflict in eastern Ukraine escalating into a full-scale invasion in 2022. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
