391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Economic sanctions are penalties used as tools of statecraft, like trade restrictions, asset freezes, and embargoes, to coerce targeted states into changing their behaviour without having to resort to military action. Despite these measures, a large number of sanctioned actors continue to evade regulations by trading natural resources illegally on the black market and evading sanctions.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Oil, gold, diamonds, lumber, rare earth minerals and other valuable goods are frequently trafficked across borders through third-party intermediaries, falsified documents, or criminal networks. Armed groups, organised crime, corruption, and continuing conflicts are often funded by the revenues made from these operations.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Sanction evasion jeopardises international security and economic stability in addition to reducing the efficacy of international measures. It is a challenge to stop illegal commerce due to weak border restrictions, little international collaboration, and insufficient enforcement methods. The UN has made bolstering international collaboration to stop resource trafficking on the black market and fight sanction evasion a top goal as geopolitical tensions rise. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Since the United Nations was founded in 1945, the use of economic sanctions has greatly increased. Sanctions were applied sparingly throughout the Cold War, but from the 1990s, they were employed more frequently to address armed conflicts, human rights abuses, terrorism and the spread of nuclear weapons. Sanctioned states and organisations created increasingly complex strategies to get around prohibitions over time.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E ( In a similar vein, terrorist groups like ISIS made substantial profits from the illicit selling of antiquities and oil. In response, the international community toughened anti-money laundering laws to monitor illegal financial flows and launched programs like the Kimberley Process Certification Scheme to lessen the trade in conflict diamonds. However, chances for illegal resource trafficking and penalty evasion have persisted due to globalisation, digital banking and increasingly intricate supply chains)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
