/E (Sanction evasion is still a significant problem for the world community today. States, corporations, and criminal groups continue to circumvent sanctions by taking advantage of flaws in banking systems, international trade, and border security. False documents, transhipment through third countries, shell corporations, or illicit shipping methods are frequently used to conceal illegal shipments.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E ( In many regions of the world, violent conflicts, corruption, environmental degradation, and violations of human rights are still fueled by the smuggling of natural resources. Illegal resource extraction frequently generates substantial income for terrorist groups, sanctioned states, and non-state armed groups, enabling them to carry out military actions in violation of international prohibitions.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (The World Customs Organisation, the Financial Action Task Force \(FATF\), the United Nations, and regional organisations are still striving to improve financial transparency, bolster sanctions enforcement, and foster global collaboration. Global efforts to stop resource smuggling on the black market and sanction evasion are still hampered by disparities in country enforcement capabilities, corruption, and a lack of information sharing. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
